virtio-gpu: host-visible blobs on 16 KiB boundaries

The host-visible region is where the host maps its own memory into the
guest for a blob the guest asked to map (Venus's ring and every mappable
device allocation). Apple silicon maps guest-physical memory in 16 KiB
pages, so the host can only satisfy a mapping whose offset and size are
multiples of 16 KiB; the allocator here hands out 4 KiB-granular nodes,
and the first ring Mesa allocates (131,268 bytes) lands on a boundary the
host cannot map, which Mesa reports as VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY from
vkCreateInstance.

Aligning the node rather than patching Mesa keeps stock distribution
images working: the guest kernel is lighter's, the container's Mesa is
whatever the image ships. The object keeps its own size; only its place
in the region is rounded, and the region is address space, not memory.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_vram.c
@@ -140,6 +140,13 @@ bool virtio_gpu_is_vram(struct virtio_gpu_object *bo)
 	return bo->base.base.funcs == &virtio_gpu_vram_funcs;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The host maps blobs in 16 KiB pages (Apple silicon); a node must start
+ * and end on one or the host cannot map it. Cheap on any other host: the
+ * region is address space, and the object's size is unchanged.
+ */
+#define VIRTIO_GPU_HOST_VISIBLE_ALIGN	SZ_16K
+
 static int virtio_gpu_vram_map(struct virtio_gpu_object *bo)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -152,8 +159,10 @@ static int virtio_gpu_vram_map(struct virtio_gpu_object *bo)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	spin_lock(&vgdev->host_visible_lock);
-	ret = drm_mm_insert_node(&vgdev->host_visible_mm, &vram->vram_node,
-				 bo->base.base.size);
+	ret = drm_mm_insert_node_generic(&vgdev->host_visible_mm,
+					 &vram->vram_node,
+					 ALIGN(bo->base.base.size, VIRTIO_GPU_HOST_VISIBLE_ALIGN),
+					 VIRTIO_GPU_HOST_VISIBLE_ALIGN, 0, 0);
 	spin_unlock(&vgdev->host_visible_lock);
 
 	if (ret)
